  that's still real — no email infrastructure needed, no OAuth provider dependency. Users are seeded by an admin command
  rather than self-signup.

  [1mLayering inside the process[0m, one module each: [38;5;153mtasks[39m (CRUD), [38;5;153mevents[39m (append-only log writer), [38;5;153mnotifications[39m (fan-out
  rules and inbox reads), [38;5;153mstream[39m (SSE connection registry), [38;5;153mweb[39m (routes and templates). The dependency arrows only point
  one way — [38;5;153mtasks[39m never imports [38;5;153mnotifications[39m; it writes an event, and [38;5;153mnotifications[39m reads events. That's what keeps
  the notification layer independently testable.

  [1mOne constraint worth stating loudly:[0m SSE fan-out lives in process memory — a registry mapping user ID to that user's
  open connections. This works because there is exactly one process. [1mIf you ever run multiple workers, live delivery [0m
  [1msilently breaks for users connected to a different worker than the one handling the write.[0m The fix is a shared bus
  (Redis pub/sub) and it's not hard, but it's not free either. I'd deploy single-worker and write this constraint into
  the spec so it isn't rediscovered as a bug. Notifications themselves are never lost regardless — they're in the
  database; only the [3mlive push[0m depends on this.

